## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Fix (5 minutes)

Replace the vulnerable dynamic XML loading with a hardcoded configuration registry that maps safe identifiers to pre-validated configuration classes.

```java
package com.example.config;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/config")
public class HardenedConfigController {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HardenedConfigController.class);

    // SECURE: Map of safe identifiers to configuration suppliers
    // No user input reaches class loading or XML parsing
    private static final Map<String, Supplier<Object>> CONFIG_REGISTRY = Map.of(
        "database", () -> new DatabaseConfig(),
        "cache", () -> new CacheConfig(),
        "messaging", () -> new MessagingConfig()
    );

    @PostMapping("/reload")
    public ResponseEntity<?> reloadConfiguration(
            @RequestParam String configId,
            @RequestHeader("X-Admin-Token") String adminToken) {

        // SECURE: Validate admin authorization
        if (!AdminTokenValidator.isValid(adminToken)) {
            log.warn("Unauthorized config reload attempt for: {}", configId);
            return ResponseEntity.status(401).body("Unauthorized");
        }

        // SECURE: Lookup by safe identifier, not by path or class name
        Supplier<Object> configSupplier = CONFIG_REGISTRY.get(configId);
        if (configSupplier == null) {
            log.warn("Unknown config requested: {}", configId);
            return ResponseEntity.status(404).body("Unknown configuration");
        }

        try {
            Object config = configSupplier.get();
            log.info("Admin reloaded configuration: {}", configId);
            return ResponseEntity.ok("Configuration reloaded: " + configId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Config reload failed for {}: {}", configId, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(500).body("Reload failed");
        }
    }
}
```

**Why this works:** The attacker never controls class names, file paths, or XML content. The registry pattern maps safe string identifiers to pre-compiled configuration classes. Even if an attacker guesses valid identifiers, they only trigger pre-approved, audited configuration code.

### Tier 2: Essential Defenses (15 minutes)

Implement a `BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor` to audit and enforce bean registration policies at container startup.

```java
package com.example.security;

import org.springframework.beans.BeansException;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.config.ConfigurableListableBeanFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.support.BeanDefinitionRegistry;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.support.BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

@Component
public class BeanDefinitionSecurityAuditor implements BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(BeanDefinitionSecurityAuditor.class);

    private static final Set<String> TRUSTED_PACKAGES = Set.of(
        "com.example.",
        "org.springframework.",
        "org.apache.tomcat."
    );

    private static final Set<String> BLOCKED_CLASSES = Set.of(
        "java.lang.ProcessBuilder",
        "java.lang.Runtime",
        "javax.script.ScriptEngine",
        "org.springframework.context.support.ClassPathXmlApplicationContext",
        "org.springframework.context.support.FileSystemXmlApplicationContext"
    );

    @Override
    public void postProcessBeanDefinitionRegistry(BeanDefinitionRegistry registry)
            throws BeansException {
        String[] beanNames = registry.getBeanDefinitionNames();

        for (String name : beanNames) {
            var beanDef = registry.getBeanDefinition(name);
            String beanClassName = beanDef.getBeanClassName();

            if (beanClassName == null) continue;

            // Check for explicitly blocked dangerous classes
            if (BLOCKED_CLASSES.contains(beanClassName)) {
                log.error("BLOCKED: Dangerous bean class detected: {} (bean: {})",
                    beanClassName, name);
                registry.removeBeanDefinition(name);
                continue;
            }

            // Verify bean comes from trusted package
            boolean trusted = TRUSTED_PACKAGES.stream()
                .anyMatch(beanClassName::startsWith);
            if (!trusted) {
                log.warn("Untrusted bean package: {} (bean: {})", beanClassName, name);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void postProcessBeanFactory(ConfigurableListableBeanFactory factory)
            throws BeansException {
        // Additional post-processing if needed
    }
}
```

**Disable bean definition overriding in all environments:**

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  main:
    allow-bean-definition-overriding: false
    allow-circular-references: false
    web-application-type: servlet

  # Restrict property sources to prevent external injection
  config:
    import: optional:classpath:application-${spring.profiles.active}.yml
```

### Tier 3: Production Hardening (30 minutes)

**Runtime bean integrity monitoring with Micrometer metrics:**

```java
@Component
public class BeanIntegrityMonitor {

    private final MeterRegistry meterRegistry;
    private int expectedBeanCount = -1;

    public BeanIntegrityMonitor(MeterRegistry meterRegistry,
                                 ApplicationContext ctx) {
        this.meterRegistry = meterRegistry;
        this.expectedBeanCount = ctx.getBeanDefinitionCount();

        // Register gauge for bean count monitoring
        Gauge.builder("spring.beans.total", ctx, ApplicationContext::getBeanDefinitionCount)
            .description("Total registered bean definitions")
            .register(meterRegistry);

        Gauge.builder("spring.beans.expected", () -> expectedBeanCount)
            .description("Expected bean count from baseline")
            .register(meterRegistry);
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 60000) // Check every minute
    public void verifyBeanIntegrity(ApplicationContext ctx) {
        int currentCount = ctx.getBeanDefinitionCount();
        if (currentCount != expectedBeanCount) {
            Counter.builder("spring.beans.integrity.violations")
                .tag("type", "count_mismatch")
                .register(meterRegistry)
                .increment();

            log.error("SECURITY ALERT: Bean count changed! Expected={}, Current={}",
                expectedBeanCount, currentCount);
        }
    }
}
```

**Integration test to lock down bean registry:**

```java
@SpringBootTest
class BeanRegistrySecurityTest {

    @Autowired
    private ApplicationContext ctx;

    @Test
    void shouldNotContainDangerousClasses() {
        Set<String> dangerousClasses = Set.of(
            "ProcessBuilder", "Runtime", "ScriptEngine",
            "ClassPathXmlApplicationContext", "FileSystemXmlApplicationContext"
        );

        for (String beanName : ctx.getBeanDefinitionNames()) {
            Object bean = ctx.getBean(beanName);
            String className = bean.getClass().getSimpleName();
            assertThat(dangerousClasses).doesNotContain(className);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void shouldOnlyContainTrustedPackages() {
        Set<String> trustedPrefixes = Set.of(
            "com.example", "org.springframework", "org.apache"
        );

        for (String beanName : ctx.getBeanDefinitionNames()) {
            Object bean = ctx.getBean(beanName);
            String pkg = bean.getClass().getPackageName();
            boolean trusted = trustedPrefixes.stream().anyMatch(pkg::startsWith);
            assertThat(trusted)
                .as("Bean %s from untrusted package: %s", beanName, pkg)
                .isTrue();
        }
    }

    @Test
    void shouldRejectDynamicConfigEndpoint() {
        // Verify the vulnerable endpoint no longer exists
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> ctx.getBean("configController"))
            .isInstanceOf(NoSuchBeanDefinitionException.class);
    }
}
```

**Key operational practices:**

1. **Baseline bean count** - Record the expected bean count per environment in CI. Alert when production bean count deviates from baseline, which could indicate classpath tampering or dependency injection attacks.

2. **Immutable container images** - Build images with `--read-only` rootfs. Prevent runtime modification of the classpath or lib directory that could inject malicious JARs.

3. **JVM SecurityManager (or module restrictions)** - On JDK 17+, use `--add-opens` restrictions to prevent reflective access to dangerous classes like `ProcessBuilder` and `Runtime`.

4. **Dependency signature verification** - Enable Maven artifact signature verification or Gradle dependency verification to ensure no tampered JARs enter your classpath.

5. **Periodic bean definition diff** - In staging, dump all bean definitions before and after dependency updates. Diff the results to catch unexpected new beans from transitive dependencies.

6. **Network egress controls** - If `ClassPathXmlApplicationContext` somehow survived to production, egress filtering prevents it from fetching attacker-hosted XML. Block all outbound HTTP from application containers except to known API endpoints.
